{
	"cell_id": 5278807111644286768,
	"cells": [
		{
			"cell_id": 8647261956871553952,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "latex",
			"cells": [
				{
					"cell_id": 12549345125790984681,
					"cell_origin": "client",
					"cell_type": "latex_view",
					"source": "\\section*{The Kerr-Newman spacetime, using $y=\\cos\\theta$}\n\nThis notebook computes curvature invariants of the Kerr-Newman metric, which\ndescribes a rotating black hole of mass $M$, angular momentum per unit mass\n$a$ and charge $Q$. We use $y=\\cos\\theta$\nas a coordinate instead of $\\theta$, so that all metric components are\nrational functions of $r$ and $y$. This substitution and the result for the\nKretschmann scalar are in R.~C.~Henry, \\emph{Kretschmann scalar for a\nKerr-Newman black hole}, Astrophys.~J.~(2000), doi:10.1086/308819,\narXiv:astro-ph/9912320."
				}
			],
			"hidden": true,
			"source": "\\section*{The Kerr-Newman spacetime, using $y=\\cos\\theta$}\n\nThis notebook computes curvature invariants of the Kerr-Newman metric, which\ndescribes a rotating black hole of mass $M$, angular momentum per unit mass\n$a$ and charge $Q$. We use $y=\\cos\\theta$\nas a coordinate instead of $\\theta$, so that all metric components are\nrational functions of $r$ and $y$. This substitution and the result for the\nKretschmann scalar are in R.~C.~Henry, \\emph{Kretschmann scalar for a\nKerr-Newman black hole}, Astrophys.~J.~(2000), doi:10.1086/308819,\narXiv:astro-ph/9912320."
		},
		{
			"cell_id": 1588447054511096840,
			"cell_origin": "client",
			"cell_type": "input",
			"source": "__cdbkernel__.display_fractions=True"
		},
		{
